 # Changelog
 
+## alpha2.3-r8 — cumulative-ACK frontier attribution
+
+- Fixed a live R7 false positive where a deliberately slow leg0 could freeze the single SMP3 v4 cumulative ACK frontier and make healthy Hy2/leg1 look like `tx_ack_stall`. Per-leg ACK writers solved write-side control HOL, but they cannot make cumulative ACK advance across an earlier missing sequence.
+- Added explicit ACK-frontier attribution to core health snapshots. The adaptive controller now treats TX ACK stall as Hy2 pressure only when the oldest unacknowledged cumulative frontier frame is currently owned by leg1. If leg0 is the earlier blocker, Hy2 is not penalized merely because later leg1 frames cannot yet retire.
+- Applied the same frontier ownership gate to the no-baseline severe TX black-hole path.
+- Improved SUSPECT diagnostics to log TX outstanding counts, leg1 outstanding count, ACK-frontier owner/age, and ACK-progress age instead of the misleading RX `pending=0 gap_age=0s` fields for a TX stall.
+- Added regressions for slow-leg0 cumulative-ACK blocking and direct snapshot verification of ACK-frontier ownership.
+- R7 per-leg ACK/control isolation, R6 lifecycle hardening, and R5 first-authenticated-leg session creation are retained. Wire/hello version remains `4`; configuration is unchanged.
+
 ## alpha2.3-r7 — per-leg control-plane isolation / graceful-close boundary
 
 - Reworked ACK emission so cumulative ACKs are published independently to each leg's writer instead of synchronously writing every carrier from one ACK loop. A blocked/black-holed leg can no longer head-of-line block ACK progress on a healthy carrier.
